\label{l:not in R}
\source{higher-siegel-weil-unitary-nonsingular-terms}
Let $D$ be an effective divisor of degree $d$ on $U$. Then $D\in U_{d}\bs \frR_{d,f}$ if and only if for all pairs of distinct points $x,y$ in the support of $D$, $f(x)\ne f(y)$.

Let $\pi_{d}: U^{d}\to U_{d}$ be the quotient map by the symmetric group $S_{d}$. We compute the divisor $\pi_{d}^{-1}(\frR_{d,f})$. Consider $U\times_{{\mathbb A}^{1}_{\ov k}} U$. Since $U$ is \'etale over ${\mathbb A}^{1}_{\ov k}$, ${\mathbb D}(U)\subset U\times_{{\mathbb A}^{1}} U$ is open and closed, hence we can write $U\times_{{\mathbb A}^{1}_{\ov k}}U={\mathbb D}(U)\coprod \frR$. For geometric points $x,y\in U$,  $(x,y)\in \frR$ if and only if $x\ne y$ and $f(x)=f(y)$. 

For $1\le i<j\le d$, let $p_{ij}: U^{d}\to U\times U$ be the projection to the $i$th and $j$th coordinates. Let 
$$\wt{\mathbb D}_{ij}=p_{ij}^{-1}(U\times_{{\mathbb A}^{1}}U)={\mathbb D}_{ij}\coprod \frR_{ij}, \mbox{ where }{\mathbb D}_{ij}=p_{ij}^{-1}({\mathbb D}(U)), \frR_{ij}=p_{ij}^{-1}(\frR).$$ 
By definition, $\pi_{d}^{-1}(\frD_{d,{\mathbb A}^{1}})=\sum_{1\le i< j\le d}\wt{\mathbb D}_{ij}$, $\pi_{d}^{-1}(\frD_{d,U})=\sum_{1\le i< j\le d}{\mathbb D}_{ij}$ as divisors on $U^{d}$. Therefore
\begin{equation}\label{Rdf sum}
\source{higher-siegel-weil-unitary-nonsingular-terms}
\pi_{d}^{-1}(\frR_{d,f})=\sum_{1\le i< j\le d}\frR_{ij}.
\end{equation}
From this we see, if $D=x_{1}+x_{2}+\cdots+x_{d}$, where $x_{i}\in U(\ov k)$, then $D\notin \frR_{d,f}$ if and only if $(x_{1},\cdots, x_{d})\notin \pi_{d}^{-1}(\frR_{d,f})$. By \eqref{Rdf sum}, the latter happens if and only if for all $1\le i<j\le d$, $(x_{i},x_{j})\notin \frR$, i.e., either $x_{i}=x_{j}$ or $f(x_{i})\ne f(x_{j})$.

