along the axes.\footnote{By this, we mean that the 
corresponding map ${\mathcal Z}^{\leq n} \underset{\bB\bG_m}\times {\rm Spec \,}(k) \to C$ is flat along the axes in the
sense of \S \ref{ss:refinement}.}

\item \label{i:z-axes-2}
\source{tates-thesis-de-rham-setting}

We have a canonical isomorphism:

\[
{\mathcal Z}^{\leq n} \underset{C/\bG_m}{\times} \bA^1/\bG_m
\simeq {\mathcal Z}^{\leq n-1}.
\]

\item \label{i:z-axes-3}
\source{tates-thesis-de-rham-setting}

Let $\iota:{\mathcal Z}^{\leq n} \to {\mathcal Z}^{\leq n}$ 
denote the map:

\[
({\mathcal L},\nabla,s) \mapsto ({\mathcal L}(1),\nabla,s)
\]

\noindent 
where ${\mathcal L}(1)$ is the line bundle on the disc 
whose sections on the disc are allowed
to have a pole of order $1$ at the base-point $0 \in {\mathcal D}$.\footnote{Another way to say this: 
$\Gr_{\bG_m}$ acts on ${\mathcal Y}_{\log}$, and the corresponding
action of $1 \in \bZ = \Gr_{\bG_m}(k)$ preserves
${\mathcal Z}^{\leq n}$ for all $n$; the induced map
${\mathcal Z}^{\leq n} \to {\mathcal Z}^{\leq n}$ is our $\iota$.}

Then $\iota$ fits into a (derived) Cartesian diagram:

\[
\xymatrix{
{\mathcal Z}^{\leq n} \ar[r]^{\iota} \ar[d] & {\mathcal Z}^{\leq n} \ar[d] \\
\bA^1 \times \bB \bG_m \ar[r]^{({\rm id},0)} & C/\bG_m.
}
\]

\end{enumerate}

\[
\xymatrix{
{\mathcal Z}^{\leq n} \ar[r]^{\iota} \ar[d] & {\mathcal Z}^{\leq n} \ar[d] \\
\bA^1 \times \bB \bG_m \ar[r]^{({\rm id},0)} & C/\bG_m.
}
\]

\source{tates-thesis-de-rham-setting}
\label{c:yn-log-cl}
