\label{lem:lflatness}
\source{pixtons-formula-abel-jacobi-picard-stack}
The map
\begin{eqnarray*}
\ell: \frak N \to \Picabs_{g,0,0}\, ,  & (f \colon C' \, \to\,  C, \mathcal{L}) \mapsto (C,\ca L)\, , 
\end{eqnarray*}
is syntomic\footnote{Flat and lci. }, and the map 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\ell': \frak N \to \Picabs_{g,0,0}\, , & \ \,(f \colon C' \to C, \mathcal{L})\,  \mapsto\,  (C',f^*\ca L)\,.
\end{eqnarray*}
is smooth.

The stack of partial stabilisations $(f\colon C' \to C)$ has smooth charts given by $\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+m}$, where $(C, p_1, \dots, p_n, q_1, \dots, q_m)$ maps to the contraction map from $C$ to the stabilisation of $(C, p_1, \dots, p_n)$ by forgetting the $q$ markings. Charts for $\frak N$ are then given by
\[\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+m} \times_{\overline{\ca M}_{g,n}} \Picabs_{\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+1}/\overline{\ca M}_{g,n}} \xrightarrow{G} \frak N.\]

Charts for the map $\ell$ are given by the composition of the top horizontal arrows in the commutative diagram
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
\square \arrow[r] \arrow[d] \arrow[rr, bend left,"\ell"]& \frak{Pic}_{\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+1}/\overline{\ca M}_{g,n}} \arrow[r]\arrow[d] & \Picabs_g\arrow[d] \\
\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+m} \arrow[r] & \overline{\ca M}_{g,n} \arrow[r] & \frak M_g\, .
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Both squares here are pullbacks, the bottom right horizontal map is smooth, and the bottom left horizontal map is syntomic. Hence $\ell$ is syntomic, using that syntomicity is a flat-local property on the target. 


Charts for the map $\ell'$ are given by commutative diagrams
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+m} \times_{\overline{\ca M}_{g,n}} \Picabs_{\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+1}/\overline{\ca M}_{g,n}} \arrow[d,"G"] \arrow[r,"F"] & \Picabs_{\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+m+1}/\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+m}} \arrow[d] \\
\frak N \arrow[r,"\ell'"] & \Picabs_g\, .
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
The right vertical arrow is a base-change of the smooth map $\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+m} \to \frak{M}_g$, so once we have shown $F$ to be smooth, we can conclude using that smoothness is a flat-local property on the target. 

In fact, $F$ is an open immersion: $F$ is isomorphic to the inclusion of the locus $$U \hra \Picabs_{\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+m+1}/\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+m}}$$ of line bundles which are trivial on the contracted rational components. 
We must verify  the induced map
$$F'\colon \overline{\ca M}_{g,n+m} \times_{\overline{\ca M}_{g,n}} \Picabs_{\overline{\ca M}_{g,n+1}/\overline{\ca M}_{g,n}} \to U$$
is an isomorphism. The source and target are smooth over $\overline{\ca M}_{g, n + m}$. On each geometric fibre over $\overline{\ca M}_{g, n + m}$, the map $F'$ is an isomorphism via the explicit description of the Jacobian of a prestable curve. But then $F'$ is  flat (by the fibrewise criterion), is unramified (by a pointwise check), and is universally injective (again by a pointwise check), and hence is an isomorphism.

